 \begin{abstract}
-We evaluate a budget-aware two-phase greedy index tuner and the Wii call-skipping mechanism (Wang et al., SIGMOD 2024) on TPC-H at scale factor 10, using PostgreSQL 18.7 with default planner settings. Wii finds indexes that improve the workload cost by 32.2\% using 429 what-if calls, against 33.4\% with 3092 calls for the same tuner without Wii (96\% of the quality with 14\% of the calls); at tight call budgets (500--1000) it is 3--7 points better than the budgeted baseline. The alpha parameter trades calls for quality smoothly, random skipping is far worse than Wii's bound-based skipping, and the cost model satisfies Wii's monotonicity and submodularity assumptions in more than 98\% of tested cases. Our implementation of Esc (Wang et al., PVLDB 2025), an early-stopping checker on top of the same tuner, saves 17--28\% of the calls at $K=20$ without measurable loss when used without Wii, but cannot stop when combined with Wii, because Wii's skipped calls leave its upper bound loose. Against the eight algorithms of the original study at about 10~GB, the tuner with Wii is within about 1 point of the best (CoPhy, DTA, Relaxation) using 429 calls where they use 1\,860--28\,102. The Wii results are single runs on one set of table statistics; the Esc results are repeated over two or three independent draws.
+We evaluate a budget-aware two-phase greedy index tuner and the Wii call-skipping mechanism (Wang et al., SIGMOD 2024) on TPC-H at scale factor 10, using PostgreSQL 18.7 with default planner settings. Over four independent statistics draws, Wii finds indexes that improve the workload cost by 32.7\% on average (32.2--33.3\%) using 429--437 what-if calls, against 33.9\% with about 3\,040 calls for the same tuner without Wii (96\% of the quality with 14\% of the calls); at tight call budgets (500--1000) it is 3.3--7.7 points better than the budgeted baseline in every draw. The alpha parameter trades calls for quality smoothly, random skipping is far worse than Wii's bound-based skipping, and the cost model satisfies Wii's monotonicity and submodularity assumptions in more than 98\% of tested cases. Our implementation of Esc (Wang et al., PVLDB 2025), an early-stopping checker on top of the same tuner, saves 17--28\% of the calls at $K=20$ without measurable loss when used without Wii, but cannot stop when combined with Wii, because Wii's skipped calls leave its upper bound loose. Against the eight algorithms of the original study at about 10~GB, the tuner with Wii is within about 1 point of the best (CoPhy, DTA, Relaxation) using 429 calls where they use 1\,860--28\,102. All Wii results are means over four statistics draws and the Esc results over two or three; the assumption and gap experiments and the comparison with the original algorithms use one draw.
 \end{abstract}
 
 \section{Setup}
 \textbf{Tuner.} The project's platform now contains a two-phase greedy algorithm (per-query greedy search, then greedy over the union of the selected indexes) running behind a cost oracle that counts and limits what-if calls. The oracle has four modes: \emph{none} (every cost question is answered by a what-if call until the budget is spent, after which the cheapest known cost of a subset of the configuration is used), \emph{Wii}, \emph{Wii + coverage} (a refinement of Wii's bounds) and \emph{random} (skips a call with fixed probability; used as a control). Wii skips a call when the lower bound $L$ on the query's cost under a configuration is within a factor $\alpha$ of the upper bound $U$ (the cheapest known cost of any subset), i.e.\ $L/U\ge\alpha$, and uses $U$ instead. The initial calls (no-index cost, and the cost with all candidates for Wii) count against the budget.
 
-\textbf{Workload and environment.} TPC-H, SF10, 19 queries (2, 17 and 20 excluded, as in the platform's baseline experiments). PostgreSQL 18.7, HypoPG 1.4.5, all planner settings at their defaults (\texttt{max\_parallel\_workers\_per\_gather}=2, \texttt{work\_mem}=4\,MB, \texttt{shared\_buffers}=128\,MB, \texttt{random\_page\_cost}=4, \texttt{effective\_cache\_size}=4\,GB). Index width up to 2; the constraint is the number of indexes $K\in\{10,20\}$ with no storage limit. Table statistics were gathered once, with \texttt{ANALYZE}, and then frozen; the no-index workload cost is 42\,625\,951.
+\textbf{Workload and environment.} TPC-H, SF10, 19 queries (2, 17 and 20 excluded, as in the platform's baseline experiments). PostgreSQL 18.7, HypoPG 1.4.5, all planner settings at their defaults (\texttt{max\_parallel\_workers\_per\_gather}=2, \texttt{work\_mem}=4\,MB, \texttt{shared\_buffers}=128\,MB, \texttt{random\_page\_cost}=4, \texttt{effective\_cache\_size}=4\,GB). Index width up to 2; the constraint is the number of indexes $K\in\{10,20\}$ with no storage limit. Each statistics draw is a fresh \texttt{ANALYZE}; we report four draws (no-index workload cost 42\,621\,151 to 42\,660\,191), of which the first (42\,625\,951) was used for the assumption and gap experiments and for the comparison in Section~4.
 
 \textbf{Metric.} Improvement $=1-\text{cost}(\text{indexes})/\text{cost}(\emptyset)$, with the cost of the final configuration obtained by fresh what-if calls for every query (not from the tuner's own, possibly derived, numbers). ``Calls'' are what-if calls issued by the oracle; repeated questions and indexes irrelevant to a query are not counted.
 
