 \section{Discussion and limitations}
 \begin{itemize}
-\item \textbf{Single statistics draw (Wii results and comparison).} Table statistics come from a random sample, and different \texttt{ANALYZE} runs move the no-index cost by about 0.1\% and the improvement of a run by up to 1--2 points (seen in earlier unfrozen runs: 32.95\% against 31.79\% for the same unlimited baseline). We froze the statistics, which makes runs repeatable but hides this spread; the conclusions above are about differences of several points, but differences below about 1--2 points (for example between alpha 0.9 and 0.95 coverage variants) should not be over-read.
+\item \textbf{Statistics draws.} Table statistics come from a random sample, and different \texttt{ANALYZE} runs move the no-index cost by about 0.1\% and the improvement of a run by about 1 point (Wii 32.2--33.3\%, unlimited baseline 33.4--34.6\% over four draws). Absolute improvements should not be read to better than about 1 point; differences between methods on the same draw, and the ordering of the methods, were stable. The assumption and gap experiments (Sections 2.3 and 2.4) and the comparison with the original algorithms (Section~4) use a single draw, and the baselines come from a different draw than our tuner.
 \item \textbf{Planner settings.} A teammate's run with \texttt{max\_parallel\_workers\_per\_gather}=8 gave a lower cost scale (no-index cost $\approx$34.95M) and, as reported to us, the same qualitative patterns; we did not verify those runs and report only the default-settings results here.
 \item \textbf{Estimated, not executed, costs.} As in the original platform, costs are optimizer estimates for hypothetical indexes; queries were not executed.
 \item \textbf{Comparison with the paper.} The paper evaluates on other DBMS and workloads, with a different cost scale, so absolute improvements are not comparable; our results agree qualitatively with its motivation (many what-if calls are redundant; skipping them by bounds keeps most of the quality; budget reallocation helps most when the budget is tight). We did not reproduce its numbers.
